{-
   `class Iterate(s -> item, resume)` - stepping an object from a body that does not know which
   producer it holds, `doc/inflight/Design-Iterator-Objects.md` §2.3.

       fn next(&self: source's) -> Maybe(source'item)
       fn resume(&self: source's, ->v: resume) -> Maybe(source'item)

   The item is **borrowed from the object**, which is what makes this one class for every producer:
   the record keeps the hand-over slot, and what a body compiled once holds between two steps is a
   loan of it. Three generated pieces say so - the instantiation `Maybe(Y)`, the origin slot on
   `self`, and the schema binding the payload position to it - and `iter(T)$item` makes the same
   statement about the erased form, one indirection out.

   What the loan buys is §3.3's stepping rule for nothing: the result is rooted in `self`, so
   stepping, moving or dropping the object while the item is live is the loan checker's ordinary
   refusal. `Reject.Class.yana` is that half.

   A **concrete** `for x in it` still honours what the producer declared - `takenLoop` in Object.yana
   moves out of the slot at `-> ->T` - and that is why both spellings exist: one signature cannot say
   "by value for some producers and by loan for others".
-}

let &trace = 0 :: Int

data Cmd = Keep | Skip

-- A producer whose passes hand a value back, which is what `resume` answers.
iter fn lexing(n: Int) -> (Int <- Cmd):
    let &i = 0 :: Int

    while i < n:
        let c = yield i
        if c is Skip then i = i + 2 else i = i + 1

data Held {id: Int}

instance Drop(Held):
    fn drop(->value: Held):
        trace = trace * 10 + value.id

{-
   A **lending** producer - `Held` owes a teardown and the hand-over borrows, so the slot holds the
   value's address. J50 gave one of these no instance of the class at all; the borrowing form gives
   it the same instance every other producer has, and `f$next` hands back what the slot points at
   rather than a copy of it.
-}
iter fn lent(n: Int) -> Held:
    let &i = 1 :: Int

    while i <= n:
        let h = Held {id: i}
        yield h
        i = i + 1

-- And an **owning** one, whose slot holds the value and whose `$held` flag says so. A generic body
-- borrows it out of the record either way, and the record drops what the consumer did not take.
iter fn owning(n: Int) -> ->Held:
    let &i = 1 :: Int

    while i <= n:
        yield Held {id: i}
        i = i + 1

data Parser(l) {lexer: l, packed: Int}

{-
   The design's `Parser` - a body generic in its lexer, stepping a **field**.

   The record stores the object inline at its real size and the call goes straight to `lexing$resume`
   once the body is specialized, which is what the class is for: a witness with two cells, and no
   handle, stack or allocation anywhere.
-}
fn (Iterate(l, Int, Cmd)) Parser.advance(&self: Parser(l)) -> Bool:
    if self.lexer.resume(Keep) is Just(t):
        self.packed = self.packed * 10 + t + 1
        return True

    return False

fn parsed() -> Int:
    let lexer = lexing(4)
    let &parser = Parser {lexer: lexer, packed: 0}

    while parser.advance():
        {}

    return parser.packed

{-
   The same body over two site types with different hand-over conventions - one lending, one owning -
   which is the whole point of the borrowing signature.

   `sum` reads the item through the loan and never takes it, so the record still owes what is in its
   slot: an owning producer's step drops the previous item as it writes the next, and its teardown
   drops the last. The trace is that order, and it is the same order the lending producer's own
   locals are released in.
-}
fn (Iterate(s, Held, {})) drain(&it: s) -> Int:
    let &sum = 0 :: Int

    while it.next() is Just(h):
        sum = sum * 10 + h.id

    return sum

fn lentDrain() -> Int:
    trace = 0
    let &it = lent(3)
    let sum = drain(it)
    return sum * 1000 + trace

fn owningDrain() -> Int:
    trace = 0
    let &it = owning(3)
    let sum = drain(it)
    return sum * 1000 + trace

fn main() -> Int:
    let a = parsed()
    let b = lentDrain()
    let c = owningDrain()

    return a + b + c
